1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,120
This video turns a grammar into code. The technique is recursive descent.

2
00:00:05,570 --> 00:00:18,413
We learn four rules that map each part of a grammar to code. Then we write and run recognizers for four question bank questions. At the end we turn a recognizer into an evaluator.

3
00:00:18,863 --> 00:00:27,503
In this course the input is always a string. A recognizer answers one question: is the string in the language? Yes or no.

4
00:00:27,953 --> 00:00:34,801
A parser also builds a tree, the A S T. A compiler outputs an executable program.

5
00:00:35,251 --> 00:00:40,072
An interpreter, or evaluator, computes a value, such as an integer.

6
00:00:40,522 --> 00:00:55,114
All four follow the same pattern. In the labs, a recognizer method returns void. The lexer throws an exception when the input is wrong. So an exception means reject, and normal completion means accept.

7
00:00:55,564 --> 00:01:06,295
Recursive descent is a way to write a parser by hand, from a grammar. Four rules do it. Rule 1. Each nonterminal becomes a method with the same name.

8
00:01:06,745 --> 00:01:17,582
Rule 2. Each terminal becomes a call to consume on the lexer. Consume takes the next token if it is the right one, and throws an exception if it is not.

9
00:01:18,032 --> 00:01:22,384
Rule 3. Each repetition, star or plus, becomes a loop.

10
00:01:22,834 --> 00:01:31,026
Rule 4. Each alternation, the bar, becomes an if statement. The if looks at the next token to choose the alternative.

11
00:01:31,476 --> 00:01:42,569
A grammar is a mathematical specification for a language. It is also a specification for the parser. With these rules, you derive the code from the grammar.

12
00:01:43,019 --> 00:01:51,105
The recognizer reads tokens from a lexer. The lexer has a position in the token stream. Here the next token is metric.

13
00:01:51,555 --> 00:02:00,515
inspect looks at the next token and returns true or false. It does not move the position. Use inspect to choose an alternative.

14
00:02:00,965 --> 00:02:09,285
consume takes the next token, if it is the expected one, and moves the position forward. Use consume for every terminal.

15
00:02:09,735 --> 00:02:29,767
If the next token is not the expected one, consume throws an exception. That is a reject. There are also forms for the end of input and for token classes, such as consume I D and consume Int. On an exam, you can assume a lexer like the one in the labs. The answers are pseudo-code.

16
00:02:30,217 --> 00:02:41,353
Question 3.1.1. A list of names. List goes to Name star. Name goes to I D, a name token from the lexer. Write a recognizer.

17
00:02:41,803 --> 00:02:43,445
Pause the video and try it.

18
00:02:43,895 --> 00:02:48,909
Apply the rules. List is a nonterminal, so it becomes a method, List.

19
00:02:49,359 --> 00:03:03,716
The right side of List is a repetition, Name star. So List has a while loop. Inside the loop is a call to Name, because the rule uses Name. The loop runs while the next token is not the end of input.

20
00:03:04,166 --> 00:03:09,393
After the loop, consume E O F. This checks that the whole input was used.

21
00:03:09,843 --> 00:03:18,696
Name is a nonterminal, so it becomes a method too. Its right side is the terminal I D, so Name calls consume I D.

22
00:03:19,146 --> 00:03:23,562
Run the recognizer on two names, Ana and Bo. List starts.

23
00:03:24,012 --> 00:03:29,644
inspect E O F returns false. There is input left, so the loop body runs.

24
00:03:30,094 --> 00:03:36,409
List calls Name. Name consumes I D: the token Ana. The position moves to Bo.

25
00:03:36,859 --> 00:03:43,173
Back in the loop. The input is not finished, so List calls Name again. Name consumes Bo.

26
00:03:43,623 --> 00:03:47,570
Now inspect E O F returns true, and the loop stops.

27
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:54,484
consume E O F succeeds. List returns normally, so the recognizer accepts the input.

28
00:03:54,934 --> 00:04:01,697
Now the input Ana, 7. The first name is consumed as before. The input is not finished.

29
00:04:02,147 --> 00:04:03,683
So List calls Name.

30
00:04:04,133 --> 00:04:14,479
Name calls consume I D, but the next token is the number 7, not a name. consume throws an exception. The recognizer rejects the input.

31
00:04:14,929 --> 00:04:26,321
Question 3.1.2 uses the measurement grammar from question 1.1.1. Digits is a token class from the lexer. Write a recognizer.

32
00:04:26,771 --> 00:04:28,414
Pause the video and try it.

33
00:04:28,864 --> 00:04:32,875
Measurement is the start nonterminal. It becomes a method.

34
00:04:33,325 --> 00:04:51,394
Its right side has a bar, so the method has an if statement. If the next token is metric, consume metric and call DecimalNumber. Else if it is imperial, consume imperial and call FractionalNumber. Else, no alternative fits, so throw an exception.

35
00:04:51,844 --> 00:04:56,687
Measurement is the start symbol. So at the end it calls consume E O F.

36
00:04:57,137 --> 00:05:13,371
DecimalNumber and FractionalNumber have no bars. Each is a sequence: consume Digits, consume dot, consume Digits, and so on. The posted solution misspells FractionalNumber in one place. The method name must match the call.

37
00:05:13,821 --> 00:05:23,933
Run it on metric 3.875. Four tokens. Measurement inspects the next token. It is metric, so the first branch runs.

38
00:05:24,383 --> 00:05:27,583
consume metric. The position moves to 3.

39
00:05:28,033 --> 00:05:29,569
Call DecimalNumber.

40
00:05:30,019 --> 00:05:34,371
consume Digits takes 3. Then consume dot takes the dot.

41
00:05:34,821 --> 00:05:39,877
consume Digits takes 875. DecimalNumber returns.

42
00:05:40,327 --> 00:05:46,535
Back in Measurement, consume E O F succeeds. The method returns normally: accept.

43
00:05:46,985 --> 00:05:51,081
Now imperial 3 slash 8. inspect metric is false.

44
00:05:51,531 --> 00:05:56,097
inspect imperial is true. Consume it, and call FractionalNumber.

45
00:05:56,547 --> 00:05:58,637
consume Digits takes 3.

46
00:05:59,087 --> 00:06:11,397
The grammar needs a second Digits, but the next token is the slash. consume throws: reject. This grammar needs a whole number before the fraction, such as 3 7 slash 8.

47
00:06:11,847 --> 00:06:20,380
Question 3.1.3 uses the professions grammar from question 1.1.2. Write a recognizer.

48
00:06:20,830 --> 00:06:22,473
Pause the video and try it.

49
00:06:22,923 --> 00:06:37,941
ProfessionalSkills has three alternatives, so it has an if with three branches. Each alternative is a nonterminal. To choose, look at the first word: Engineer or Architect means Technical, and so on.

50
00:06:38,391 --> 00:06:46,178
If no first word fits, throw. ProfessionalSkills is the start symbol, so it ends with consume E O F.

51
00:06:46,628 --> 00:07:01,668
Technical has a group with a bar inside, so it has its own if. Engineer is followed by calculates. Architect is followed by draws. After the group, consume design. Medical and Legal have the same shape.

52
00:07:02,118 --> 00:07:18,374
The posted solution misspells calculates in the consume call. With that typo, the correct sentence Engineer calculates design is rejected. A python copy of our code accepts exactly the five sentence patterns of the grammar.

53
00:07:18,824 --> 00:07:24,968
Run it on Engineer cuts patient. inspect Engineer is true, so the first branch runs.

54
00:07:25,418 --> 00:07:26,677
Call Technical.

55
00:07:27,127 --> 00:07:30,476
Technical inspects Engineer again, and consumes it.

56
00:07:30,926 --> 00:07:43,811
Now Technical needs calculates. The next token is cuts. consume throws, and the recognizer rejects the sentence. This matches the stuck derivation in question 1.1.2.

57
00:07:44,261 --> 00:07:51,536
Why does one inspect call choose the right branch? Each alternative of Measurement begins with a different token.

58
00:07:51,986 --> 00:07:56,573
So one token of lookahead decides. Anything else is a reject.

59
00:07:57,023 --> 00:08:12,553
Grammars where one token of lookahead always decides are L L one. Left to right, leftmost derivation, one token of lookahead. Recursive descent works directly for them. Video 19 shows the full test.

60
00:08:13,003 --> 00:08:22,283
A recursive descent recognizer always works on the leftmost unfinished part first. So its calls follow the leftmost derivation.

61
00:08:22,733 --> 00:08:40,867
Question 3.2.1. An L L one calculator grammar. It is a left-factored form of the grammar in question 1.2.1. Write a recognizer, then an evaluator. Note the epsilon alternatives in TermTail and FactorTail.

62
00:08:41,317 --> 00:08:42,959
Pause the video and try it.

63
00:08:43,409 --> 00:08:50,300
First, the new part: an epsilon alternative. TermTail goes to plus Expr, or to epsilon.

64
00:08:50,750 --> 00:08:57,001
If the next token is a plus, take the first alternative: consume the plus and call Expr.

65
00:08:57,451 --> 00:09:10,656
Otherwise take epsilon. Epsilon consumes nothing, so the method does nothing and returns. In code, that is an if with no else. The caller then goes on with the same next token.

66
00:09:11,106 --> 00:09:23,074
S, Expr and Term have one alternative each. Each is a sequence of nonterminals, so each method is a sequence of calls. Expr calls Term, then TermTail.

67
00:09:23,524 --> 00:09:38,009
Factor has two alternatives. An open parenthesis starts the second one. So: if the next token is an open parenthesis, consume it, call Expr, and consume the close parenthesis. Else consume an Int.

68
00:09:38,459 --> 00:09:43,153
TermTail and FactorTail are the epsilon pattern: an if with no else.

69
00:09:43,603 --> 00:10:04,893
The posted S method calls only Expr. It does not check the end of input. Then the input 1 plus 2, close parenthesis, is accepted, which is wrong. Add consume E O F, as in the earlier questions. A python check compared our recognizer with the grammar on all strings up to 7 tokens.

70
00:10:05,343 --> 00:10:16,437
Run the recognizer on 1 plus 2. S calls Expr, Expr calls Term, and Term calls Factor. Factor inspects for an open parenthesis. There is none.

71
00:10:16,887 --> 00:10:19,233
So Factor consumes the Int, 1.

72
00:10:19,683 --> 00:10:27,619
Term calls FactorTail. The next token is a plus, not a times. So FactorTail takes epsilon and returns.

73
00:10:28,069 --> 00:10:34,811
Expr calls TermTail. The next token is a plus, so TermTail consumes it and calls Expr.

74
00:10:35,261 --> 00:10:39,165
The new Expr calls Term, then Factor, which consumes 2.

75
00:10:39,615 --> 00:10:44,329
FactorTail and TermTail see the end of input. Both take epsilon.

76
00:10:44,779 --> 00:10:50,326
All calls return, and S calls consume E O F. It succeeds: accept.

77
00:10:50,776 --> 00:10:59,523
Draw every call of that run as a tree. Each call is a node, and its children are the calls it makes and the tokens it consumes.

78
00:10:59,973 --> 00:11:03,685
A method that took epsilon has no tokens below it.

79
00:11:04,135 --> 00:11:19,388
This tree has the same shape as the parse tree of 1 plus 2 in the grammar. A python check compared the two trees. So a recognizer builds the parse tree in its calls, even if it never stores it. A parser stores it.

80
00:11:19,838 --> 00:11:35,475
Now the evaluator. Start from the recognizer and make two changes. Each method returns an int, and each method does the computation. Expr returns Term plus TermTail. Term returns Factor times FactorTail.

81
00:11:35,925 --> 00:11:40,981
Factor returns the value inside the parentheses, or the Int it consumes.

82
00:11:41,431 --> 00:11:57,986
TermTail returns the value of the Expr after the plus. For epsilon, it must return something that does not change the sum. That is 0, the identity for plus. FactorTail returns 1 for epsilon, the identity for times.

83
00:11:58,436 --> 00:12:03,236
The evaluator computes while it parses. It does not build an A S T.

84
00:12:03,686 --> 00:12:11,622
Test the evaluator on 2 times 3 plus 4. The answer should be 10. Factor consumes 2 and returns 2.

85
00:12:12,072 --> 00:12:20,328
FactorTail sees a times. It consumes it and calls Expr, as the grammar says: FactorTail goes to times Expr.

86
00:12:20,778 --> 00:12:23,658
Inside that Expr, Factor returns 3.

87
00:12:24,108 --> 00:12:27,329
TermTail sees the plus and calls Expr again.

88
00:12:27,779 --> 00:12:29,422
Factor returns 4.

89
00:12:29,872 --> 00:12:37,488
Now the values come back up. The inner Expr returns 3 plus 4, which is 7. FactorTail returns 7.

90
00:12:37,938 --> 00:12:44,615
So Term returns 2 times 7, which is 14. The evaluator returns 14, not 10.

91
00:12:45,065 --> 00:12:59,295
The cause is in the grammar of the question. FactorTail goes to times Expr. So after a times, the whole rest of the input, 3 plus 4, becomes one Expr. The evaluator computes 2 times 7.

92
00:12:59,745 --> 00:13:20,694
Our fix: FactorTail goes to times Term. Then the times takes only the next Term, 3. The value is 2 times 3, plus 4, which is 10. In the code, FactorTail returns Term instead of Expr. Question 1.2.1 has the same idea: Term goes to Factor times Term.

93
00:13:21,144 --> 00:13:39,448
Both grammars accept the same strings. They are weakly equivalent, from video 16. So the recognizer is correct either way. Only the trees differ, and the evaluator follows the tree. A python check ran both versions on all strings up to 7 tokens.

94
00:13:39,898 --> 00:14:01,637
The notes have a job interview question. Evaluate exponents, with integers and parentheses. Exponentiation is right associative: 2 to the 3 to the 2 is 2 to the 9, which is 512. The grammar is right recursive and left factored. Tail goes to caret Number, or epsilon.

95
00:14:02,087 --> 00:14:16,679
The code follows the same rules. Number calls Base, then Tail, and returns base to the power exp. Tail consumes a caret and returns Number. For epsilon it returns 1, because x to the 1 is x.

96
00:14:17,129 --> 00:14:27,518
Base returns the value in parentheses, or the integer. This code uses an array and a position instead of a lexer, but the technique is the same.

97
00:14:27,968 --> 00:14:35,712
Run it on 2 caret 3 caret 2. The caret is the exponent sign. Base consumes 2 and returns 2.

98
00:14:36,162 --> 00:14:39,767
Tail sees a caret. It consumes it and calls Number.

99
00:14:40,217 --> 00:14:45,721
The second Number gets base 3. Its Tail sees another caret and calls Number again.

100
00:14:46,171 --> 00:14:55,131
The last Number gets base 2. Its Tail sees the end of input and returns 1. So that Number returns 2 to the 1, which is 2.

101
00:14:55,581 --> 00:14:59,037
The middle Number returns 3 to the 2, which is 9.

102
00:14:59,487 --> 00:15:08,277
The first Number returns 2 to the 9, which is 512. The right recursion makes the operator right associative.

103
00:15:08,727 --> 00:15:21,783
The notes list common mistakes for these questions. The grammar does not accept a single integer. The associativity is wrong. Or the grammar has common prefixes, so it is not L L one.

104
00:15:22,233 --> 00:15:31,299
Another mistake is to build an explicit A S T in an evaluator. That makes the code larger. Compute the value while you parse.

105
00:15:31,749 --> 00:15:40,603
From the posted solutions in this video: check the end of input in the start method, and spell every terminal exactly as in the grammar.

106
00:15:41,053 --> 00:15:47,581
In an evaluator, an epsilon alternative returns the identity element of its operator.

107
00:15:48,031 --> 00:15:58,271
To summarize. A nonterminal becomes a method. A terminal becomes consume. A repetition becomes a loop. An alternation becomes an if.

108
00:15:58,721 --> 00:16:08,811
inspect looks at the next token without moving. consume takes it, or throws. An exception means reject, and normal completion means accept.

109
00:16:09,261 --> 00:16:16,387
An epsilon alternative becomes an if with no else. The start method ends with consume E O F.

110
00:16:16,837 --> 00:16:22,170
The calls follow the leftmost derivation, so the call tree is the parse tree.

111
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:35,036
For an evaluator, each method returns a value, and an epsilon alternative returns the identity element. Next video: ambiguity, precedence and associativity.
